  "selected": [
    {
      "id": "eof-modes-and-pdo-index",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/eof-modes-and-pdo-index.md",
      "sha256": "3544f2f552ecd0f41c41eb09edf7012f49ac8421900f2b571b8498d37f2726b1",
      "reason": "The question is exactly this fragment's subject — the PDO as the leading EOF of North Pacific SST anomalies, with area-weighted EOFs, a conventional sign, an explained variance that depends on grid/domain/record, and an amplitude read from the regression pattern on the standardised index, compared between sources with an area-weighted correlation.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "The mode's amplitude is the regression pattern's area-weighted RMS in °C per standard deviation (anomalies regressed on the standardised index), never read off the EOF; explained variance is comparable only on one common grid over one period.",
        "`compute_pdo_index` fixes the arbitrary EOF sign by convention (positive = cool central/western North Pacific); compare patterns with `analyze_correlation` using `weighting='area'`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices.md",
      "sha256": "e0afb5c187aca1e075f9549153b1e896320bb0539544f66d3293fab4b4b77c92",
      "reason": "The PDO is defined on anomalies relative to a 1950-2014 monthly climatology, and this fragment fixes that an anomaly is only defined relative to its baseline and that mixing seasons or baselines offsets the series, which constrains how the indices are built and compared.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Each model index must come from its **own** climatology (`compute_anomaly_vs_own_climatology`); subtracting an observed climatology puts the model's mean-state bias into the index.",
        "Comparing with the published PDO requires its `base_period`: series on different baselines differ by one offset per calendar month, and the answer must state the climatology and baseline used."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "common-comparison-mask",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/common-comparison-mask.md",
      "sha256": "bbb98200f52b4a692ce097704b8c438179a795a44bec6b856c03cf31931af4ea",
      "reason": "The required spatial correlations between each model's regression pattern and the ERSSTv5 regression pattern exist only over cells the two share, so a common mask must be built and applied to the pattern comparison.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Regression patterns of a variability mode are the documented exception to a shared mask: correlate each with the ERSSTv5 pattern over the cells the two share (`analyze_correlation` drops cells either one lacks) instead of masking all patterns to common coverage first.",
        "Any shared mask built here needs `ocean_only=False` (the reference is not WOA23); report the cell counts alongside the metrics."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-tos-bulk-top-cell",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "concepts/cmip6-tos-bulk-top-cell.md",
      "sha256": "4acdeba0f49784d4435dbc4eed34ca9777254313daa951641759fa02df24cf64",
      "reason": "The model field being turned into a PDO proxy is CMIP6 `tos` (top-cell bulk temperature, also under ice), not a skin SST, so comparing it with ERSSTv5 needs that definition stated.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Call `compute_pdo_index` with `depth_level=None` (top level reproduces the model's own SST definition); a `depth_level` of 10 m averages two levels in GFDL-ESM4 but one in CESM2 and MPI-ESM1-2-LR."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-native-grid-vs-regridded",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-native-grid-vs-regridded.md",
      "sha256": "8ff046ffbadc5794fcd2f8fe93d3bb947c736dec650aa3a3cd6e2412b7da8e68",
      "reason": "The question specifies bilinear regridding onto a 1-degree grid for the models, and this fragment says regridding changes masks and conservation and should be used only for maps and pattern comparisons, which is exactly the use here.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "An EOF mode is the exception to native-grid numbers: put all four sources, ERSSTv5 included, on the common grid before the EOF and take its explained variance and regression pattern there.",
        "State the regridding method wherever a pattern correlation is quoted: the question prescribes bilinear, while the fragment calls 'conservative' the right choice going to a coarser grid and before any area-weighted step — that difference is stated in the answer."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-grid-labels",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "datasets/cmip6-grid-labels.md",
      "sha256": "e349f9e305621b6c10b35cccd0490da521cc7e3ba08bfb53890479f81024d117",
      "reason": "CESM2 and MPI-ESM1-2-LR are on native curvilinear grids while GFDL-ESM4 is already conservatively regridded to 1°, which affects the mask, the regridding step and which operations are exact.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "GFDL-ESM4 `tos` is `gr`, already regridded to 1° (conserve_order1), so its \"native\" land–sea mask is the regridded one; CESM2 (100 km) and MPI (250 km) differ ~2.5× in resolution, so amplitudes of narrow features are not comparable."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "serial-correlation-and-significance",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/serial-correlation-and-significance.md",
      "sha256": "fe11bd008257a589f44e369faba5fa31ab3cf9ad99280e88c763ac45d2b0e917",
      "reason": "The requested lag-1-year autocorrelation and the Pearson correlation with the published PDO series involve strongly autocorrelated monthly series, so effective sample size and the inflation of correlations by shared trends must be reported.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Monthly ocean series are strongly autocorrelated: quote correlation significance from the effective sample size that `analyze_correlation` applies by default, not from the number of months.",
        "The ERSSTv5-derived index and the published PDO are two estimates of one quantity: correlate them as they are (no detrending); the correlation shows agreement in time only, not equal variance or amplitude."
      ]
    }
